<!DOCTYPE html>

<html lang="en"><head><link href="../assets/tailwind.css" rel="stylesheet"/><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1" name="viewport"/><title>Attendance vs. Labor Data: The Difference Between Knowing When Someone Worked and What They Worked On | ZKTeco WFM</title><meta content="A punch can tell you when an employee worked. Labor data explains what those hours meanâ€”job, department, project, cost center, worktag or activity. Enterprise time collection should capture the right level of context without turning the employee into an accountant." name="description"/><link href="/assets/site-enhancements.css?v=keytitles-v126" rel="stylesheet"/><link href="../assets/insight-enhancements.css" rel="stylesheet"/><link href="../assets/privacy.css" rel="stylesheet"/><link href="../assets/insight-standard-v92.css" rel="stylesheet"/><link href="../assets/layout-standard-v114.css" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="canonical" href="https://zktecowfm.com/insights/attendance-vs-labor-data" /></head><body class="insight-page time-labor-data-editorial"><Header /><section class="article-hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="crumb"><a href="../thought-leadership">Thought Leadership</a> / Time &amp; Labor Data</div><div class="cat">Time &amp; Labor Data</div><h1>Attendance vs. Labor Data: The Difference Between Knowing When Someone Worked and What They Worked On</h1><p>A punch can tell you when an employee worked. Labor data explains what those hours meanâ€”job, department, project, cost center, worktag or activity. Enterprise time collection should capture the right level of context without turning the employee into an accountant.</p><div class="meta"><span>ZKTeco WFM Insights</span><span>Educational Perspective</span><span>Updated September 2026</span></div></div></section><main class="content-wrap"><article class="article-body"><p class="lead">Time and labor are related, but they are not the same data problem. Attendance answers <strong>when</strong>. Labor answers <strong>where the time belongs.</strong> Organizations that need accurate job costing, departmental allocation, project tracking or position-specific labor need both.</p>
<div class="tl-flow"><div><span>Attendance</span>Who?</div><div><span>Attendance</span>When?</div><div><span>Labor</span>What work?</div><div><span>Labor</span>Where allocated?</div><div><span>Outcome</span>Cost + reporting</div></div>
<h2>A perfect punch can still produce the wrong business result</h2><p>An employee can clock in at 7:00 AM and out at 3:30 PM with flawless timestamps. If those eight hours were split across two departments, three jobs or a project that needs separate costing, attendance alone does not provide enough information.</p><p>This distinction matters because Workday Time Tracking can use worktags to categorize worker hours for costing, reporting and downstream processes. Workday documentation specifically describes cost centers, projects, locations and other worktags as time-entry dimensions.</p>
<div class="tl-scenario"><div class="eyebrow">Operating example</div><h2>Eight correct hours. Wrong labor.</h2><p>A maintenance technician spends four hours on Plant A equipment and four hours supporting a capital project. The employee's IN and OUT punches are correct. If the project transfer is missing, payroll may still pay eight hours correctlyâ€”but finance may charge all eight hours to the wrong cost center.</p><p>That is why time accuracy and labor accuracy must be evaluated separately.</p></div>
<h2>How much labor detail should the employee enter?</h2><p>The answer depends on what the organization genuinely needs at the point of work. There are four common models.</p><table class="tl-table"><tr><th>Model</th><th>Best fit</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Attendance only</strong></td><td>Hours are attributed automatically and employees rarely change jobs or departments.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Employee-selected labor</strong></td><td>The employee is the best person to know which job, department or activity applies.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>System-derived labor</strong></td><td>Schedule, position, assignment or other enterprise data already determines the correct value.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Default + exception</strong></td><td>Most time follows a normal allocation, but employees occasionally transfer or override.</td></tr></table>
<h2>Do not make employees navigate the enterprise data model</h2><p>A Workday tenant may contain hundreds or thousands of valid worktag values. That does not mean those values belong on a shared time clock.</p><p>The employee should see a <strong>filtered, understandable set of choices</strong> based on role, location, assignment or prior selection. Workday supports allowed values, defaults and related-worktag behavior in time entry; the same UX principle should guide the workforce edge.</p>
<div class="tl-overlook"><div class="eyebrow">What most buyers overlook</div><h2>Labor data affects more than payroll</h2><p>Depending on the organization, labor context can influence project costing, financial reporting, departmental expense, grants, job costing, approval routing and management analytics. A technically valid punch can therefore be financially wrong even when gross hours are right.</p></div>
<h2>Design the transaction around the operating event</h2><p>Instead of asking â€œWhich fields should the clock show?â€, start with the actual work transition:</p><ul><li>An employee starts a normal shift.</li><li>A worker transfers to another department.</li><li>A technician moves to a new job.</li><li>A nurse begins work at a different location.</li><li>A multi-position employee changes roles.</li></ul><p>Then decide which labor context can be defaulted and which must be explicitly captured.</p>
<div class="tl-grid">
<div class="tl-card"><h3>Attendance question</h3><p><strong>Did the employee work from 7:00 AM to 3:30 PM?</strong> If yes, the time record may be complete for pay purposes.</p></div>
<div class="tl-card"><h3>Labor question</h3><p><strong>What did the employee do during those 8.5 hours?</strong> That answer can determine department, project, job, cost center or other labor allocation.</p></div>
</div>
<h2>The same hours can produce very different business answers</h2>
<table class="tl-table"><tr><th>Workforce example</th><th>Attendance alone shows</th><th>Labor context adds</th></tr>
<tr><td>Manufacturing operator</td><td>8 hours worked</td><td>5 hours Line 1 + 3 hours Line 3</td></tr>
<tr><td>Warehouse associate</td><td>8 hours worked</td><td>Receiving + Picking + Inventory</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maintenance technician</td><td>8 hours worked</td><td>Routine maintenance + Capital Project 417</td></tr>
<tr><td>Healthcare employee</td><td>12 hours worked</td><td>Facility / department / position context</td></tr>
<tr><td>Field employee</td><td>9 hours worked</td><td>Customer A + Customer B + travel or other approved activity</td></tr></table>
<p>The examples are intentionally simple. The important point is that the <strong>same attendance record can support multiple labor stories</strong>. If the business needs the labor story for costing or operations, it has to be captured or derived somewhere.</p>
<div class="tl-scenario"><div class="eyebrow">Failure mode</div><h2>Reconstructing labor after the shift</h2><p>A supervisor receives a timecard with eight correct hours but no project transfer. The next morning, the employee is asked to remember when the work changed. Was it 10:00, 10:20 or after lunch? The longer the organization waits, the more the correction depends on memory rather than the original operating event.</p><p>This is why source capture matters. When the employee is the best person to know that work changed, the cleanest moment to record it is usually <strong>when the work changes</strong>.</p></div>
<h2>Separate what the system knows from what the employee knows</h2>
<p>Not every labor value needs employee input. A worker's normal department, location, position or default assignment may already be known. In that case, asking the employee to select it again adds friction without adding information.</p>
<p>A useful design exercise is to classify every labor dimension into one of three groups: <strong>known and defaulted</strong>, <strong>derived from enterprise context</strong>, or <strong>employee-selected because only the employee knows the answer at that moment</strong>.</p>
<div class="tl-overlook"><div class="eyebrow">What most buyers overlook</div><h2>Attendance accuracy can hide labor inaccuracy</h2><p>A clean-looking timecard is not necessarily a clean labor record. Organizations that evaluate only missed punches and total hours may overlook misallocated labor that later appears in project costing, departmental expense, productivity reporting or management analytics.</p></div>
<div class="tl-rule"><div class="eyebrow">Decision rule</div><strong>Capture labor context only when it changes the business result.</strong><p>Default what is already known, derive what the system can determine reliably, and ask the employee only for the information that must be known at the point of work.</p></div>
<h2>Questions leaders should ask</h2><div class="questions"><ul><li>Which downstream decisions depend on labor allocation?</li><li>What labor data must be captured at the point of work?</li><li>What can be derived from Workday rather than selected by the employee?</li><li>How many choices will an employee see at one time?</li><li>Can relevant values be filtered by worker, site, job or role?</li><li>What happens when the labor structure changes during the day?</li><li>How are offline labor selections handled?</li><li>How are incorrect allocations corrected and audited?</li></ul></div>
<div class="tl-rule"><div class="eyebrow">Decision rule</div><strong>Capture only the labor detail that changes the business result.</strong><p>Default what is known. Ask the employee only for information the system cannot reliably infer.</p></div>
<div class="perspective"><div class="label">ZKTeco WFM perspective</div><h2>Capture when the employee workedâ€”and what the work belonged to.</h2><p>ZKTeco WFM is designed for organizations whose workforce events carry more meaning than a simple IN or OUT. Through Ultima time clocks and TimeTrack, the employee interaction can be configured to capture the labor context that matters to the customerâ€”such as approved jobs, departments, cost centers or other labor selectionsâ€”without forcing every worker to navigate the full complexity of the enterprise structure.</p><p>The design principle is to bring the right choices to the employee at the moment the work changes. That can make labor allocation more timely and reduce the need for supervisors or payroll teams to reconstruct the day later from memory. For Workday customers, CirrusDCS provides the Workday-specific collection and integration layer behind those interactions; for other software-partner architectures, ZKTeco WFM can support the approved combination of applications, middleware, APIs or SDKs appropriate to that platform.</p><p>This is where a workforce-data-collection specialist adds value: <strong>the endpoint, employee workflow and integration are designed together</strong>. Attendance records when work happened. Labor data explains what those hours mean to the business. A strong collection strategy should be able to do both where the operating model requires it.</p></div><div class="takeaway"><h2>Key takeaway</h2><p>A punch tells the enterprise when someone worked; labor context tells it what those hours belonged to. If that context is important for costing, projects, departments or operations, capture it as close as practical to the moment the work occurs. The goal is not to burden the employee with accounting complexity. It is to present only the relevant choices, create a clean workforce event and give downstream systems better data to process.</p></div>
